  "solutions": [
    {"ordinal": 0, "title": "Check Every Pair", "intuition_md": "There are only $\\binom{n}{2}$ pairs of positions. Try each one and keep those that sum to `target`. The listing order doesn't matter, and `i < j` holds by construction when the inner loop starts after `i`.", "algorithm_md": "For each `i`, for each `j > i`: if `nums[i] + nums[j] == target`, record `[i, j]`. With 32-bit values, add in a wider type so the sum can't overflow.", "code": {"python": "def pairs_to_target(nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[list[int]]:\n    found = []\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(nums)):\n            if nums[i] + nums[j] == target:\n                found.append([i, j])\n    return found\n", "rust": "fn pairs_to_target(nums: Vec<i32>, target: i32) -> Vec<(usize, usize)> {\n    let mut found = Vec::new();\n    for i in 0..nums.len() {\n        for j in i + 1..nums.len() {\n            // Widen before adding: two i32s can sum past i32::MAX.\n            if nums[i] as i64 + nums[j] as i64 == target as i64 {\n                found.push((i, j));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    found\n}\n"}, "time_complexity": "O(n^2)", "time_complexity_reason": "Every pair of positions is checked once.", "space_complexity": "O(1)", "space_complexity_reason": "Beyond the output, which can itself be $\\Theta(n^2)$ when every value is equal."},
    {"ordinal": 1, "title": "Remember Earlier Positions", "intuition_md": "Fix the later position `j`. Its partners are exactly the earlier positions holding `target - nums[j]`. If a map from value to the positions seen so far is maintained while scanning, those partners are one lookup away, and each pair is found exactly once, at its larger index.", "algorithm_md": "1. Keep `earlier`: value $\\to$ list of positions already scanned.\n2. For each `j`: every `i` in `earlier[target - nums[j]]` gives the pair `[i, j]`. Then add `j` to `earlier[nums[j]]`.\n3. Adding `j` *after* the lookup is what keeps a value from pairing with itself.", "code": {"python": "def pairs_to_target(nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[list[int]]:\n    earlier: dict[int, list[int]] = {}  # value -> indices seen so far\n    found = []\n    for j, x in enumerate(nums):\n        for i in earlier.get(target - x, []):\n            found.append([i, j])\n        earlier.setdefault(x, []).append(j)\n    return found\n", "rust": "use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nfn pairs_to_target(nums: Vec<i32>, target: i32) -> Vec<(usize, usize)> {\n    // Keyed by i64: the partner `target - x` can fall outside i32's range.\n    let mut earlier: HashMap<i64, Vec<usize>> = HashMap::new();\n    let mut found = Vec::new();\n    for (j, &x) in nums.iter().enumerate() {\n        let partner = target as i64 - x as i64;\n        if let Some(indices) = earlier.get(&partner) {\n            for &i in indices {\n                found.push((i, j));\n            }\n        }\n        earlier.entry(x as i64).or_default().push(j);\n    }\n    found\n}\n"}, "time_complexity": "O(n + p)", "time_complexity_reason": "One expected-$O(1)$ lookup per position, plus $p$, the number of pairs reported.", "space_complexity": "O(n)", "space_complexity_reason": "The map holds every position once (plus the output)."}
  ]
